[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Behavior Trees and State Machines in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When developing AI for games in Unity, choosing the right architectural pattern is critical. Finite State Machines (FSMs) and Behavior Trees (BTs) are two of the most popular approaches for controlling NPC logic. This tutorial explores both systems, their differences, and how to implement them effectively."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Finite State Machines (FSM)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An FSM consists of a set of distinct states (such as Idle, Patrol, Attack, Flee) and rules for transitioning between them. FSMs are intuitive for simple behaviors, but they can quickly become difficult to maintain as complexity grows due to the exponential increase in transition logic."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic FSM Implementation in C#"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;

public enum AIState { Idle, Patrol, Attack }

public class SimpleFSM : MonoBehaviour
{
    public AIState currentState;

    void Update()
    {
        switch (currentState)
        {
            case AIState.Idle:
                HandleIdle();
                break;
            case AIState.Patrol:
                HandlePatrol();
                break;
            case AIState.Attack:
                HandleAttack();
                break;
        }
    }

    void HandleIdle()
    {
        // Check conditions to switch to Patrol
        currentState = AIState.Patrol;
    }

    void HandlePatrol()
    {
        // Patrol logic here
    }

    void HandleAttack()
    {
        // Attack logic here
    }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Behavior Trees"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Behavior Trees solve the scaling issues of FSMs by breaking logic down into a hierarchical tree of nodes. They execute top-to-bottom and left-to-right, evaluating conditions and executing actions. Key node types include Composites (Sequence, Selector), Decorators (Inverter, Repeater), and Leaves (Action, Condition)."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts of Behavior Trees"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Selector Node: Executes children until one succeeds. Useful for priority-based decision making."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sequence Node: Executes children in order until one fails. Useful for multi-step tasks."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Action Node: Performs a concrete game action and returns NodeState (Success, Failure, Running)."},{"order":13,"type":"heading","text":"When to Use Which?"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use Finite State Machines for simple, discrete states like menus, animation controllers, or straightforward object states (e.g., Door Open, Door Closed). Use Behavior Trees for complex AI characters that require concurrent behaviors, modular design, and easy scalability."}]
